%% Erlang inet resolver config for reaching a LOCAL commonplace serve over
%% BEAM distribution. Consumed via ERL_INETRC (see bin/commonplace-mcp).
%%
%% WHY THIS EXISTS (2026-08-05)
%%
%% epmd and the serve's distribution listener are bound to loopback
%% (ERL_EPMD_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1, -kernel inet_dist_use_interface {127,0,0,1}).
%% But the serve runs under a SHORT name, `commonplace_dev@commonplace`, and
%% this host's `commonplace` does NOT resolve to 127.0.0.1 — there is no
%% /etc/hosts entry for it, so the native resolver answers with the machine's
%% real interface addresses (including its PUBLIC IP). A client therefore
%% asks epmd at an address epmd does not listen on, gets `noport`, and
%% concludes the serve is not running.
%%
%% That failure is badly disguised: the MCP escript's refuse-without-serve
%% contract fires and prints "no running `commonplace serve` node found"
%% while the serve is perfectly healthy and answering HTTP. It reads as an
%% MCP bug and is actually name resolution.
%%
%% TWO NON-OBVIOUS RULES, both established by testing the full matrix rather
%% than the first plausible cause (bare `erl`, 2026-08-05):
%%
%%   1. The `{lookup, ...}` line is REQUIRED. Without it the file's host
%%      entries are never consulted and the native resolver wins.
%%
%%   2. Both names MUST share ONE `{host, ...}` entry. Two separate entries
%%      mapping the SAME address clobber each other, and the earlier name
%%      stops resolving — so ADDING a second line for `localhost` to an
%%      otherwise-working file BREAKS `commonplace`. That is the opposite of
%%      what you would expect from an additive config change.
%%
%%   host only ................................. FAILS
%%   host + separate localhost entry ........... FAILS
%%   host + lookup ............................. works
%%   host + separate localhost entry + lookup .. FAILS
%%   both names in one host entry + lookup ..... works  <- this file
%%
%% ERL_INETRC is read at VM BOOT, so editing this file does nothing for an
%% already-running process. Restart the client (or the serve) to pick it up.
{host, {127,0,0,1}, ["commonplace", "localhost"]}.
{lookup, [file, dns]}.
